- Curvature at each point - Menger curvature
- Speed cap at each point - use lateral limits and speed limits
- Account for acceleration and deceleration - use deceleration and acceleration limits
- Calculate total time of Lap - calculus :(


keywords:

TOTG - time-optimal trajectory generation
Motion profiling - (s curve, trapezoidal) - finding speeds with given constraints
velocity limit curve / admissible velocity - explains how curvature limits max speed at any point
banga's algorithm , kunz & stilman algorithm - famous algorithms for time optimal path parameterisation

geometric limits - lateral acceleration limits , max V = sqrt(Max lateral acceleration x radius of curvature)
kinematic limits (linear) - hard limits on top end speed (Vmax) and max acceleration/braking (Amax)

actually solving:
Forward-Backward integration (Classical approach):
	you have a forward pass - start at beginning of path, accelerate as hard as possible respecting curvature limits
	then a backwards pass - start at end of path, work backwards decelerating as hard as possible
	minimum merge - at each point along the path, take the lowest speed from forward pass, backward pass and geometric curve limit (also max speed)

for better modelled tyre limits, use 'GG Diagram envelope'

S-Curve / trapezoidal profiles:
with a straight line or simple segments, reduce complexity:
trapezoidal path - assume instant acceleration, speed goes up linearly, flats out at max speed, the steps down linearly
s-curve - bounds jerk (rate of change of acceleration) creating s shaped velocity curve

libraries and code examples:
ROS2 - look up documentation for nav2_smoother or time optimal trajectory generation tutorials in MoveIt
WPILib - great documentation on TrapezoidProfile and swerve Drive Kinematics constraints

already made software:
offline calculation for a predefined track:
OpenLap - lap time simulator with forward-backward integration with predefined track -> optimal velocity and acceleration profiles
Pacejka Magic Formula - for tyre modelling, finding true acceleration limits:

real time planning:
global planner part - mapping cones and updating path
local planner part - following path safely and at speed

Model predictive control - industry standard real time tracking, handles constraints whilst deciding control inputs
kinematic bicycle model - useful model to start with
model predictive contouring control - advanced model used by zurich driverless team
AMZ driverless autonomous racing - great zurich paper
MIT team apparently have lots of older open source stuff

minimum curvature path - for making a geometric racing line
